e like where there might be  gaps in the schedule that you maybe want  to fill in or  red days that you'd maybe want to avoid  and so i'll get kind of into the impact  and kind of how this all played out but  first i want to kind of talk about how  we built this app  why we built this app in r and kind of  how we kind of use  the electronic medical record data at  chop to go from  you know the the medical record itself  to into r  so you know every day you have the  schedulers who are scheduling in the  medical record itself  and then every night all of that data  goes into our data warehouse  not only the schedule the scheduled data  but all of like the hospital data for  that day  goes into our data warehouse um and  as an analyst we're writing sql queries  against that data warehouse to build up  sort of  um the data set that you need for an  analysis in my case  i need to know the scheduled surgical  admissions for the next four months by  unit  and you know by day um and so our data  engineers  at shop have um set up apache airflow  which we kind of use as like analyst  owned etl  and this lets analysts um at chop who  are  sort of the content experts create and  own data marts in the data warehouse  with little to no data engineer  involvement  and so airflow kind of runs my scheduled  admission sql query every morning  and then creates a table in the data  warehouse of just those results for me  so every day this is updating for the  next four months it's sort of like a  rolling table  and so that table now exists in the data  warehouse as its own like standalone  table  and that's the table that i that i bring  into our it's like almost set up it has  all the raw data that i need and just  the data i need  um but i need to bring it into r to kind  of organize it a little bit and get it  ready to present  so i bring it into our studio and i use  a combination of  some tidy verse packages namely like d  plier and lubridate  as well as the rocky package which none  of you have ever heard of and i'll get  into that in a sec  to kind of organize and get the data  ready um the app i showed earlier was  actually a flex dashboard  which is kind of just like a fancy r  markdown document with some shiny under  the hood powering the reactivity  and it kind of provides us like a nice  simple layout for everyone to use  um all of the charts that you that you  saw were made with the high charter  package  it's a really great package for creating  interactive charts in r it's like  they're very sharp and very snappy and  they look great like right out of the  box  and that's allows the reactivity with  that hovering tool tip i showed you can  kind of customize that  and they're actually heat maps just  designed to look like calendars and  we're able to kind of discretely color  code in  the days kind of using that heat map  style  and then finally it's published on our  studio connect which allows anyone in  the institution with like  the link to go to the app and see what  this is  and so to get into rocky i couldn't come  to our studio and not talk about rocky  which is chop's internal package that we  developed to help us gather  explore analyze and present data and  it's called rocky because  the office that i'm in used to be called  the office of clinical quality  improvement or ocqi  and then we had r o c q i and then rocky  and then we're based in philly and then  the rocky thing  just kind of snowballed and it's called  rocky  um and we have a nice little hex sticker  there with uh with our rocky holding up  the queue  so like i said it's available to anyone  in the organization and it's like really  easy  to install we have it hosted on a cran  like repository within shop  so that we don't have to install it from  github we just have we edited everyone's  our profiles to kind of point to this  repository to install which is great  so how did i use that in this specific  instance well i really just used it to  get the data  from our cdw to r so before  if you're kind of familiar with getting  data from databases you kind of have to  do a few steps right you have to set  your connection you have to like  reference your sql file you have to  execute the sql file you have to  disconnect from the database and then  you can start doing all of your stuff  with rocky and specifically the run sql  file that we implemented all of that  sort of abstracted away from the  analysts  and i just have to remember one function  and where i saved the file  and this is great for like a couple  reasons um  you know as a new hire coming in you  don't have to have all of these steps  that you're  as you're learning everything else and  then you have to learn these like little  steps to connect the database we kind of  abstract all that away  and secondly there's no more passing  around this like sacred connection  string from user to user  or from yourself copying and pasting it  from document to document right it's  like  just one function to remember it's very  easy so  this is the end of my rant but if you  have like the time i definitely  recommend like developing in our package  internally to help solve some of these  like  institutional or like administrative  problems um  so kind of getting back to the app so  why did we use r for this and not like  the medical record software itself  um so the medical record software might  be kind of preferred because it's in the  scheduler's workflow  like they're in that software doing  their work but  it's kind of a closed environment and we  can't develop in there easily or quickly  it would require like outside we require  outside resources of the other  developers time  but with r you know we can do this quick  iterative tests of change and invite  the users to the table and do this sort  of design thinking approach where we're  kind of asking how do you guys want to  see it  and the other great thing is if it turns  out not to be useful we don't mind just  walking away from it because it was very  quick and easy to start up and you can  imagine starting a whole  big development project with outside  resources and then halfway through  saying actually never mind like that's  not going to feel great  but with this because our startup costs  are so low we can get things up and  running and then  you know quickly pivot if it's not  working out so the other good  reasons we did this in our is that we  have full control you know we can  have control as on rcdr studio connect  in terms of who can access it  what it looks like so the app in its  final version that i showed you it  looked nothing like that at the start  but because we invited the schedulers to  come and look at it we kind of got their  feedback and we were able to make some  changes  and finally it kind of serves now as a  single source of information we don't  have any like more conflicting reports  um that people may be generating  themselves in the medical record  you know you can inadvertently be  filtering for things you're not aware of  and you know data doesn't quite match up  and  it's it kind of solves that problem um  so as far as like the impacts of the app  so remember like before this app there  wasn't really a place to see this  information in one place and and now  there is  and while our initial audience really  was like the surgical schedulers and at  the point of scheduling  um we had other like operational leaders  like medical directors and nurse  managers kind of picking up on this  and finding it useful um these were  these were like leaders who were used to  seeing things maybe  24 to 48 hours in advance kind of this  very near-term planning  but now they're able to see at least the  surgical admissions you know up to four  months in advance  and it kind of took on life as this new  like early warning system  so now rather than trying to um maybe  influence how things are being scheduled  we're showing like these the decision  makers how things are  already scheduled and having them react  to it to be able to kind of plan for  potentially high admission  or you know high occupancy days ahead of  time um and this became kind of apparent  um one day last may i started to get  emails  from these from a nurse managers and  medical directors at the period medical  director saying you know  we have a day two weeks from now where  we have 22 admissions to the floor  which is like way too high and we need  to start thinking about rescheduling  or maybe canceling some cases which is  like a big deal we never want to do that  you never want to cancel an elective  case especially for like capacity  reasons like  that's not even like a medical reason to  reschedule something which is like  we never want to do that but if we have  to do that  we should probably be doing it as far in  advance as we possibly can  it's better to do it two weeks in  advance than two days in advance even  though obviously we never want to do it  um so i was like sure i can get you this  data here's a patient list here are  those 22 patients  here's their you know here's the  surgeon's doing it have your  you can now start the conversations do  stuff and so they were able to kind of  tweak that day and then i started  thinking  well that's happened once it's probably  going to keep happening and i should set  up a system so that i'm not sort of  responding to these  as their as these days are coming up so  to kind of get around that  um and to do that i scheduled these  dynamic emails with r  and so basically based on a threshold an  email gets sent off to these operational  leaders with a patient list  for these problematic high occupancy  days so that if they need to take action  they can  and so it kind of gives them the  information when they need it and only  when they need it  um so there's an example here with some  of the patient information blacked out  but you know it's very simple it's just  the pd  um it's an html document that's attached  to an email says here's the patients for  this day  and it just gives them the information  to act if they need to  and so kind of between this email and  the app i showed we have  these operational leaders really making  like daily operational decisions  using these like workflow tools that we  made with in r  and i think it really highlights the  fact that r is more than just um you  know  an analysis kind of language or you know  for modeling like you can really make  workflow tools that are used every day  and it's been really kind of rewarding  to have people use this stuff  so why did we use r for the emails well  really it's all about the conditional  sending of the email so there's a code  chunk on the screen  um the very end of my script there's  just a simple if statement that checks  if  if there's data in a table and that  table above was basically says if a  threshold is met  show me the patience if it wasn't met  show me nothing so  if that table has data in it we send the  email and if we don't we just suppress  sending the email and that's that's it  it's really simple to set up  and with with connect it's really simple  to kind of schedule this so this is an  example of like the scheduling pane so i  just schedule the  the report to refresh daily and i send  the email after the update and that's  sort of it  and so i hear that people are using this  a lot and there was a day where we had  eight admissions to the picu which  was like wasn't going to happen so they  were able to use this  this this email to identify who those  patients were in they were able to  successfully reschedule two of them  ahead of time which kind of made that  day flow like a lot easier  um so kind of for like big takeaways  here  um i think as we all know but it's  really highlighted in some of this work  is that like r really allowed us to have  this fast iteration and user testing  um and while the initial audience was  those surgical schedulers and we were  really trying to like narrow down that  variability  um we didn't really have sort of like  the tools  to or a parallel process outside of that  app to really like  provide a lot of governance to that and  make that really stick and that  sort of this project really highlighted  that like who's in charge of saying like  no  like that wasn't obvious before this and  this kind of brought that to light like  if we have more admissions than we want  like who makes that call is it surgery  is it anesthesia is it you know hospital  operations like  what do we do here so that was kind of a  bad and good thing that came out of this  app and we're actually circling back to  address that now  um these workflow tools are really  allowing people to take  action and make decisions um because for  the first time all of this information  is kind of  exposed throughout the hospital um and  what i'm finding is like seeing this  data in this new way this like long time  horizon is now leading to the kind of  new questions being asked of the data um  we're seeing the schedule now four  months in advance and we're thinking  how can we take what we're seeing and  schedule new admissions in a way that  really does smooth that variability out  um and then to to that end and to the  the next steps here really about  intentionally scheduling things  you know and to do that we think we need  to be able to try to be able to predict  some sense of like have some sense of  the future hospital occupancy  um both the scheduled and the emergency  merchant population  uh we need to align all the scheduling  processes which are very different  between surgical and non-surgical  and then we think if we can do all of  that we can almost provide like a  recommend recommendation system to the  schedulers  for like the quote best day to schedule  that admission  from an operations point of view and  then there's obviously lots of other  things to consider you know  is the patient available is a surgeon  available doesn't need to happen within  a certain window  but we think as a starting point like we  definitely have the opportunity  to provide some decision support to the  schedulers  using all of all of the all this data  that we now have access to  and i've been showing people so you know  fingers crossed but maybe next year i'll  be back here talking about  the results of this project so um thank  you for  coming and if you have any questions and  also we're hiring so come find me  afterwards if you are interested in  joining the team thank you thank you  so we do have a number of questions  first  from matt given that the occupancy is  highly variable  does the historical data provide a  relatively conviction  consistent picture year over year  i mean there's definitely some seasonal  patterns in terms of like we have  the surgical and the medical populations  are kind of flipped so high surgical is  like very high in the summer time  because  we're at children's hospitals the kids  are off school they're having all their  elective procedures then  um it kind of dips in the winter but at  the flip side the flu season  sign of speak peaking in the winter so  there is definitely some seasonal and  like year-over-year  like larger longer-term trends but day  to day it is very variable  so nick asks in high is high occupancy  important in the picu and are there  departments  where you don't want to see high  occupancy  for example it could be an indicator of  something else that's wrong  i don't know i think we we never really  want a unit to be  fully maxed out you always want some  wiggle room um as far as something else  going on  i think there's definitely some  opportunity to look at has there been  changes in like the length of stay for  any certain population or procedure  in each unit and that's something we're  considering too when we're thinking  about like  scheduling patients in the future is  like thinking about length of state so  that's something we'll definitely have  to look into  so steven asked given that surgical  scheduling is highly decentralized  how do you incentivize or force surgical  schedulers to use your app in their  workflows  good question um you have to get  somebody  really important involved  uh it is hard it's really hard um i  think  for better or worse we never really had  to think about it until now because now  we're so packed we have to start making  these decisions so  this this um the lack of governance was  sort of hidden behind the fact that  we never kind of needed to worry about  it in the past but now that we do it's  kind of forcing  us to deal with it for sure so uh  gabriel asked do you adjust for length  of surgery  and ortho surgery can last 12 plus hours  others can be much shorter  we need to know how many hours of  surgery are scheduled  that's a good point i think when it  comes to to suggesting a day  to have the case you have to make sure  that there is a  there's the or time available for sure  so like uh  uh like a like i don't know like ear  tubes like take very short but like  orthopedic surgeries take very long so  you have to  factor in not only the patient  availability and the surgeon  availability but like  that time and the projected length of  state so there's lots of  definitely lots of things we need to  think about for sure  tiffany has a technical question here is  there a way to have a connect  r studio plus jupiter authenticate the  uh lms a canvas via lti for example  similar to jupiter hub i don't know many  of those words so i don't know  i'm sorry well  it wasn't just me  all right um so last question does  rescheduling happen very often for  surgeries  not often um and when it does it's  usually  because of like a medical reason um  the patient's sick they they maybe ate  food too close to the surgery we have to  reschedule it  so we do track the reasons why cases are  being rescheduled  um and so that's actually one of the  things we want to use that sort of like  a metric here is like how often are we  are we canceling or rescheduling cases  due to capacity reasons specifically  so luckily we do have the ability to  track that um so it doesn't happen often  but  when it does we are able to at least  know why so well thank you well let's  thank brandon  and all of our speakers
 

